---
title: Approach
description: The therapeutic approach at Ankersted
---
At Ankersted, sessions start with you and what you want to work on. Together we explore what feels present, what you need, and what might help you move forward.

**Acceptance and Commitment Therapy – building flexibility and direction**

I work primarily from Acceptance and Commitment Therapy (ACT), a more recent evidence-based cognitive approach. I combine talk therapy with knowledge of the body's stress responses and the nervous system. My approach is attentive and practical, and along the way we can use concrete exercises, visual models, and biofeedback when that is useful for you.

In therapy we can work toward greater flexibility in meeting what is difficult. It is not about thinking positively or getting rid of hard feelings, but about having more options for acting in the direction of the life you want to live.

**Focus on body, mind, and nervous system**

Psychological strain can be felt throughout the body. Thoughts, feelings, and the body's stress response affect one another, so it can help to understand what happens in the body when we are under pressure.

I am certified in biofeedback, which can make the body's responses more concrete and visible. Although our primary work is through conversation, I can draw on my knowledge of biofeedback and the nervous system to understand how strain shows up for you—and to explore what might help your body find more calm.

Biofeedback is a tool we can include if it makes sense for you.

**Understanding neurodivergence**

I have done substantial work with assessment and psychological testing. That has given me a solid understanding of how neuropsychological challenges, such as ADHD and autism, can affect everyday life.

For some people it is a great relief to see why certain situations become so demanding—when the demands of the environment create strain, and there is a need to work purposefully on strategies that fit your particular conditions.

**The way forward**

In our sessions we explore what burdens you, what works, and which strategies can create better balance in your life.

I help create a clear frame for our work, and together we figure out what is meaningful to focus on and work with. Sometimes we need to understand something that has happened. Other times we need concrete tools, or practice doing something differently.

We adjust the pace and focus as we go, so the therapy fits you.
